{
  "dom_id": "step-t2262",
  "name": "Introduction",
  "problem": "There are lots of different types of quadrilaterals. Let's look at some examples.\n\n- A **trapezoid** is a quadrilateral with exactly ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14151358/assets/95836ae470c61ab15bfb67d6f0c92d2ad8d03d400b04473e81e58de3ee44bf32.svg) pair of opposite sides that are parallel to each other. \nThe parallel sides are called the **bases**. The other two sides are called the **legs**.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14151358/assets/9fa09c46a2119d35c796065a696954931e20062fd5b3677a5484bef92bc2b0d8.png)\n\n- An **isosceles trapezoid** is a  trapezoid with congruent legs.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14151358/assets/b6b2b2012c1d724f9da8b23551ec4f978f190d86875d7db7a3074635d7f40c02.png)\n\n- A **parallelogram** is a quadrilateral with ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14151358/assets/f5f3224583db57b9cfe323c8489ef5e5271000002b5ea253eb98fd067a858b2d.svg) pairs of parallel sides.\n\n \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14151358/assets/5d0f383f3510f54b415a7ab99ef52fb30270924f97b8afec59534a96cd3edcef.png)\n\n Opposite sides and opposite angles of a parallelogram are always congruent. Moreover, if a quadrilateral has ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14151358/assets/f5f3224583db57b9cfe323c8489ef5e5271000002b5ea253eb98fd067a858b2d.svg) pairs of opposite congruent sides or  ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14151358/assets/f5f3224583db57b9cfe323c8489ef5e5271000002b5ea253eb98fd067a858b2d.svg) pairs of opposite congruent angles, it is always a parallelogram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14151358/assets/39ccf26333785f9ef47eed68a96bc40005a6ba025fdb6e250beefe14f10b043d.png)\n\n- A **rectangle** is a parallelogram where all angles are right.\n\n \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14151358/assets/55a16e0ba93bf1f01d576cca558805aaea9631d1fa8e43b149509d4f8eb4c834.png)\n\n A rectangle where all sides are congruent is a **square**.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14151358/assets/a04b2a0e86be8b085c5e3fc906d5aa66dfea45c033d52cd4746031202a7d427d.png)\n\n- A **rhombus** is a parallelogram where all sides are congruent. The opposite angles of a rhombus are also congruent.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14151358/assets/d3588d7cbe8f285198df3909df3c03fdec04f53a3f75769e46a0ab4283fa27b3.png)\n\n A rhombus where all angles are congruent is a **square**.\n\n \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14151358/assets/941872050d5407bcd9cb532e318276adccff802230ed854a2d292666fc0096fc.png)\n\n- A **kite** is a quadrilateral with two pairs of congruent sides that are adjacent to each other.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14151358/assets/116e323d81ee190c0f69b4d7cdcefe9f0952da883b0d6d3c6d4036119794baa7.png)",
